For a function f defined on the positive integers (or on the positive reals), let f^{(k)} denote the k-fold iterate of f, i.e. f^{(1)}(x) = f(x) and f^{(k)}(x) = f(f^{(k-1)}(x)) for k >= 2. Consider the following classical arithmetic functions:

- sigma(n) = sum of the positive divisors of n;
- sigma_r(n) = sum of the r-th powers of the positive divisors of n;
- J(x) = pi(x) = the number of primes p <= x;
- J(x; a, b) = the number of primes p <= x with p congruent to a modulo b;
- omega(n) = the number of distinct prime divisors of n;
- d(n) = tau(n) = the number of positive divisors of n;
- p(n) = the largest prime divisor of n (with the convention p(1) = 1).

For each of the following, given fixed parameters, determine (as explicit functions of those parameters, or with sharp asymptotics) the smallest positive integer k such that:

(a) sigma^{(k)}(n) > m, where n, m are given positive integers with n >= 2;
(b) J^{(k)}(x) = 1, where x >= 2 is a given real number (so one iterates the prime-counting function starting from x until reaching 1);
(c) omega^{(k)}(n) = 1, where n >= 2 is a given positive integer;
(d) d^{(k)}(n) > m, where n, m are given positive integers;
(e) g^{(k)}(n) = 1, where g(n) = p(n) - 1 and n >= 2 is a given positive integer (i.e., the depth of the iteration n, p(n)-1, p(p(n)-1)-1, ... until reaching 1).

Provide analogous determinations when sigma is replaced by sigma_r and when J(x) is replaced by J(x; a, b) with gcd(a, b) = 1.

